---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { LAST_UPDATED, AUTHOR_NAME, CONTACT_EMAIL, AUTHOR_ROLE } from '../../data/site-config';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { personSchema, organizationSchema, breadcrumbSchema, profilePageSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
import { displayDate } from '../../lib/format';
import { PAGES } from '../../lib/pages';
import { P } from '../../lib/engine/params';
const lang = 'en';
const n = PAGES.length + 1;
const nSrc = Object.keys(P.sources).length;
---
<PageLayout title="Who works out the gold melt values on US Gold Karat, and how" description="Who publishes US Gold Karat, why it turns the live gold price into the melt value of American karat gold and silver, and why it is not a gold buyer or a dealer." lang={lang} jsonLd={[personSchema(lang), organizationSchema(lang), profilePageSchema({ path: route('about', lang), lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path: route('about', lang) }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <header class="mb-6"><h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-navy-900 md:text-4xl">Who works out the values on US Gold Karat</h1></header>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <div class="not-prose mb-6"><div class="text-lg font-semibold text-navy-900">{AUTHOR_NAME}</div><div class="text-navy-700">{AUTHOR_ROLE[lang]}</div></div>
    <p>US Gold Karat is published by Radif Partners, a company that builds calculators on public data. It is for people in the United States who have gold or silver to sell, to value or to buy: a drawer of broken 10K chains, a 14K class ring, 22K bangles brought from abroad, a few Gold Eagles, a sterling flatware set nobody uses.</p>
    <h2>Why US Gold Karat exists</h2>
    <p>Search for the price of 14K gold per gram and the first results are run by gold buyers. They print their own buying price, often with no date and no spot price beside it, so you cannot tell what share of your gold they are paying for. This site does the opposite: it shows the melt value from the karat fraction and the live spot price, with the time of that price, and turns any offer into a percentage of it. It prints no buyer’s price and ranks no buyer.</p>
    <h2>Where the figures come from</h2>
    <p>Karat fractions and the meaning of gold filled and plated come from the FTC Guides for the Jewelry Industry (16 CFR part 23); stamping tolerances from the National Gold and Silver Stamping Act; the grain, pennyweight and troy ounce from NIST; coin contents from 31 U.S.C. 5112; tax rules from the Internal Revenue Code and IRS guidance; bullion sales tax from each state’s statute. The parameter file cites {nSrc} federal pages, read on {displayDate(P.retrieved_at, 'en-US')}, and each state page links to its own statute. The spot price comes from gold-api.com. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> gives every formula and its limits.</p>
    <h2>What we are not</h2>
    <p>We are an independent site, not a gold buyer, a pawn shop, a refiner or a dealer. We do not buy, sell, store, test or appraise gold, we arrange no sales and we take no commission. Nothing here is financial or tax advice: a question about your own return goes to a tax professional.</p>
    <h2>How the site is paid for</h2>
    <p>It is free and needs no account. If advertising is added one day, it will never sit above a calculator and cannot use what you type, because nothing you type leaves your browser. No buyer or dealer can pay to be mentioned.</p>
    <h2>Tell us when a figure is wrong</h2>
    <p>Write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with the page, the values you entered and the source you rely on. The <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains how a correction is made. The site has {n} indexed pages, listed in the footer.</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-navy-600">Rules last read on <time datetime={LAST_UPDATED}>{displayDate(LAST_UPDATED, 'en-US')}</time>.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
